{
  "dom_id": "step-q236846",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "If an arithmetic sequence has $f(15)=-4$ and common difference $d=-5$, find the value of $n$ such that $f(n)=-184.$\n\n$\\,n=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f(15)=-4$ is $14$ jumps after $f(1),$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(15) & =f(1)+14d \\\\ -4 & =f(1)+14(-5) \\\\ -4 & =f(1)-70 \\\\ 66 & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =66+(n-1)(-5) \\\\  & =66-5n+5 \\\\  & =71-5n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $f(n)=-184,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =71-5n \\\\ -184 & =71-5n \\\\ -255 & =-5n \\\\ n & =\\frac{-255}{-5} \\\\ n & =51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-184$ is the $51$st term of the sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
